% ============================================================================
% earth-layer-matter-behavior.query.adj — worked recall over the geology/
% chemistry causal-composition library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "why does the outer core
% behave the way it does?" — it states no fact of its own — it only
% `import`s the grounded rule and asks binding queries. The answer is
% DERIVED, composing Earth-layers' specific state assignment with
% states-of-matter's general chemistry principle for that state, so every
% answer carries BOTH citations in its `steps` trail.
%
% Placed at the stdlib PACKAGE ROOT (not beside
% `geology/earth-layer-matter-behavior.adj`) — the same cross-directory-
% import sandboxing pattern `season-start-month-number.query.adj` already
% established: the library itself lives in `geology/` and reaches its
% chemistry sibling via a relative `../chemistry/states-of-matter.adj`
% import, which only resolves if the CONSUMING entry program (this file)
% sits at or above the package root, so both `geology/` and `chemistry/`
% fall inside the CLI's sandboxed import subtree.
%
% Run (from this directory, the package root, so the relative import
% resolves):
%     ../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli earth-layer-matter-behavior.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "geology/earth-layer-matter-behavior.adj"

? earth_layer_matter_behavior(outer_core, $B)   % expect takes_shape_of_container (direct: the outer core is liquid)
? earth_layer_matter_behavior($L, fixed_shape)  % expect inner_core (reverse: which layer holds a fixed shape?)
? earth_layer_matter_behavior(crust, $B)        % expect abstain -- the crust is "rigid", not one of chemistry's three keyed states
